# Python
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
*.pyo
*.pyd
.Python
env/
venv/
.venv/
*.egg-info/
dist/
build/

# Node
node_modules/
npm-debug.log*
yarn-error.log*
*.tsbuildinfo

# Environment variables.
# Deny everything, then re-admit only the templates. The previous rules listed
# `.env`, `.env.local` and `.env.*.local`, which left `.env.production` — the
# file holding the real OPENAI_API_KEY and POSTGRES_PASSWORD — perfectly
# committable. An allowlist fails safe: a new .env.<something> is ignored by
# default instead of needing someone to remember to add a rule.
.env
.env.*
!.env.example
!.env.*.example

# Terraform.
# .tfvars holds the owner's home IP (personal data) and is never committed;
# the .example next to it is. Local state files would leak resource ids and,
# for some providers, secrets — state lives in S3, not here. The .terraform
# directory is a multi-hundred-MB provider cache.
.terraform/
.terraform.lock.hcl
*.tfstate
*.tfstate.*
*.tfvars
!*.tfvars.example
crash.log

# OS
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db

# Office temp/lock files
~$*

